{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791448996,"nonce":"X99Tedq4K9LX4e1PAw+9rNWIXkk=","message":"Landed in 7bc52ae96b622db080d3407c6b36ecaf0feb82de (Cranelift: reduce floating-point pressure across calls).\n\nThe production pass runs after egraph elaboration, with no second optimizer run or Wasmtime dependency change. It moves only scalar f32/f64 arithmetic, within a block, after its latest available operand definition and before intervening calls, when at least two distinct operands have a single global use. Global use counting resolves aliases and includes successor arguments. Loads, effects, traps, branches, shared operands and rematerializable constants retain their ordering. Both ordinary function compilation and OSR helpers use the same path. Tier policy remains interpreter-first; performance runs had LLVM off.\n\nCode quality: native fixture tests on ARM64 and x86-64 require six FP spills to become three, for both f32 and f64. Actual NUC lua$main$3 changes from 24 spill stores + 24 reloads to 12 + 12 across its four typed arms; frame 512 -\u003e 464 bytes, VCode 973 -\u003e 937 lines. nbody advance and its OSR helper keep the same native counts and frame sizes.\n\nCorrectness: 428 compiler unit tests; 1 aggregate snapshot, 6 load-region, 14 OSR transfer and 1 promoted-stub integration tests pass on both architectures. Lua 43 and Python 50 conformance categories pass on both architectures with LLVM off and on; three official Lua suites excluded. All 17 Lua and 22 supported Python benchmark checksums pass on the NUC. New scheduling tests cover IEEE values across a call that overwrites the inputs, aliased output, shared and duplicate operands, late loads, constants and chained/FMA dependencies across multiple calls.\n\nNUC fixed-binary controls, seven randomized processes per arm, affinity 0-7: short operator (100k loop iterations) wall 49.250 ms saved baseline / 49.248 disabled / 49.230 enabled. Long original operator: 211.450 / 212.012 / 234.403 ms medians. These long samples are bimodal in every arm, including the unchanged saved baseline; preserve them and do not claim a win from this batch. A separate eleven-process-per-arm confirmation pinned to CPU 0 is stable: long wall 227.020 / 227.403 / 223.614 ms, kernel 192.928 / 192.887 / 188.699 ms. Enabled improves wall 1.67% versus disabled in that diagnostic; short wall 56.349 / 56.879 / 57.302 ms is roughly flat. The different affinity must remain explicit. All operator samples have zero thermal counter increments.\n\nARM64 same-binary controls (7 per mode): long operator wall 195.751 -\u003e 189.676 ms (3.1%), kernel 162.521 -\u003e 155.575 ms. ARM Python timings became highly variable; no Python performance claim derives from them. Other NUC same-binary controls are mostly flat: nbody -n50 wall 322.903 -\u003e 323.550, float -n5 100.691 -\u003e 100.924, FFT 491.341 -\u003e 491.173, Lua nbody 204.233 -\u003e 204.382, method_call 122.715 -\u003e 121.821 ms. FFT is slower versus the saved binary even with the pass disabled, so that between-build difference is not credited to this transform. No all-kernel improvement claim.\n\nCompile-cost probe (200 interleaved samples after 10 warmups, synthetic CLIF, CPU 0): for 3/30/300 sums, stock median 27.220/173.364/2888.523 us versus scheduled 24.640/129.094/1752.965 us. These are backend fixture compile times, not frontend startup measurements. A discarded first version placed arithmetic immediately before the call and raised allocator cost for wide batches; placing it after the last operand removed that regression.\n\nNo Cargo/build processes observed in 614 primary + 210 confirmation one-second monitor samples or pre/post-run checks; binary hashes unchanged. Background desktop/browser work averaged approximately 0.55 CPU core. One saved-baseline Lua nbody sample has a thermal flag, retained; no other primary/confirmation sample does. Sources and executables froze before timing, with no builds between samples.\n\nArtifacts: /tmp/zyntax-clif-schedule-final.json locally; primary raw data /tmp/zyntax-clif-schedule-controls on NUC and /tmp/zyntax-clif-schedule-controls-nuc locally; confirmation /tmp/zyntax-clif-schedule-op-pinned on NUC and its -nuc local copy; native /tmp/zyntax-clif-schedule-audit-{normal,disabled} on both. ARM /tmp/zyntax-clif-schedule-arm-controls. Compile cost /tmp/zyntax-clif-schedule-cost-nuc.log; probe source /tmp/zyntax-clif-schedule-cost.rs.\n\nThe scheduling defect is fixed. Long-run throughput gains are modest; the kernel retains two temporary object allocations per loop iteration. The existing operator timing-variance issue 27d96d2c7a0dfd477a554f3cfc872182b9ff0248ce87c5773bc985b4592eec38 remains open.","files":null}]}